        "---\n",
        "title: \"Graphs and Language\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Dependency, knowledge, and message passing\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "Language is not only a sequence.  A sentence has syntactic relations, a document collection has citation and hyperlink structure, a knowledge base has subject-relation-object triples, and a corpus has co-occurrence patterns among words.  Graphs provide a mathematical language for representing these relational structures.  This chapter develops graph-based NLP from a clean mathematical point of view: nodes, edges, matrices, Laplacians, walks, propagation, and message passing.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.1 Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After reading this chapter, students should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- represent linguistic objects as graphs;\n",
        "- convert graphs into adjacency, degree, incidence, and Laplacian matrices;\n",
        "- distinguish directed, undirected, weighted, bipartite, heterogeneous, and relational graphs;\n",
        "- construct word co-occurrence graphs, dependency graphs, document-term graphs, and knowledge graphs;\n",
        "- interpret graph walks, centrality, PageRank, and label propagation mathematically;\n",
        "- derive one layer of graph neural message passing;\n",
        "- explain graph convolution, graph attention, and relation-specific message passing;\n",
        "- implement small graph algorithms from scratch in Python;\n",
        "- use AI tools to audit graph construction choices rather than simply generate graph code.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Guiding question\n",
        "When should an NLP problem be represented as a graph rather than as a sequence or a bag of words?\n",
        "\n",
        "A graph is useful when the relational structure is part of the signal.  The important mathematical decision is not \"use a graph neural network\" but first: choose nodes, choose edges, choose weights, and decide what invariances the representation should have.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.2 From sequences to relational objects\n",
        "\n",
        "Earlier chapters represented a text as a sequence\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_{1:T}=(w_1,\\\\ldots,w_T)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "over a vocabulary $V$, or as a vector $x\\in R^d$.  These representations are powerful but not always natural for relational information.\n",
        "\n",
        "A graph representation starts with a set of nodes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal V = \\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and a set of edges\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal E \\subseteq \\mathcal V \\times \\mathcal V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In NLP, nodes may represent:\n",
        "\n",
        "- tokens in a sentence;\n",
        "- word types in a vocabulary;\n",
        "- documents in a corpus;\n",
        "- entities in a knowledge base;\n",
        "- concepts in an ontology;\n",
        "- users, questions, courses, or resources in an advising system.\n",
        "\n",
        "Edges may represent:\n",
        "\n",
        "- adjacency in a sentence;\n",
        "- syntactic dependency;\n",
        "- word co-occurrence;\n",
        "- citation or hyperlink;\n",
        "- semantic relation;\n",
        "- membership of a word in a document;\n",
        "- retrieval similarity.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus a graph is not just a data structure.  It is a modeling assumption about which relations are relevant.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Graph modeling decision.**  A graph-based NLP model begins with a map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{text data} \\longmapsto G=(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal E,W,X),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $W$ stores edge weights and $X$ stores node features.  The quality of the downstream model depends strongly on this construction.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.3 Basic graph definitions\n",
        "\n",
        "An undirected weighted graph on $n$ nodes is described by a symmetric adjacency matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A\\in R^{n\\times n},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "A_{ij}=A_{ji}\\ge 0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here $A_{ij}>0$ means that nodes $i$ and $j$ are connected, and $A_{ij}$ gives the edge weight.  For an unweighted graph, $A_{ij}\\in \\{0,1\\}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "A directed graph uses a possibly nonsymmetric adjacency matrix.  Then $A_{ij}>0$ may mean an edge from node $i$ to node $j$.  The direction convention must be stated because different software packages use different conventions.\n",
        "\n",
        "The weighted degree of node $i$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_i = \\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The degree matrix is diagonal:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D=\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\ldots,d_n).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For undirected graphs, the combinatorial graph Laplacian is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L=D-A.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The normalized adjacency matrix is often written\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "S=D^{-1/2} A D^{-1/2},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the normalized Laplacian is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L_{\\mathrm{norm}} = I-D^{-1/2}AD^{-1/2}=I-S.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "When isolated nodes occur, $d_i=0$, and the inverse degree factors must be handled carefully.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.4 Why the Laplacian matters\n",
        "\n",
        "For a scalar function $f\\in R^n$ on nodes, the quadratic form of the Laplacian is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f^T L f\n",
        "= \\frac{1}{2}\\sum_{i,j=1}^n A_{ij}(f_i-f_j)^2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This identity is central.  It says that $f^T L f$ is small when connected nodes have similar values.  In NLP, $f_i$ may be a label score, a semantic score, or one coordinate of a node embedding.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "## Smoothness prior on graphs\n",
        "A graph Laplacian encodes the prior that connected nodes should have similar predictions or representations.  For example, if two documents are very similar, a classifier may penalize assigning very different label scores to them.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "The Laplacian also appears in spectral clustering.  Eigenvectors associated with small eigenvalues reveal low-frequency graph signals, meaning functions that vary slowly across edges.  This is the graph analogue of using low-frequency Fourier modes to describe a smooth signal.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.5 A sentence as a dependency graph\n",
        "\n",
        "A dependency parse represents a sentence by directed grammatical relations.  For example, consider\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "language models predict tokens\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "A possible simplified dependency graph is:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "predict -> models\n",
        "models  -> language\n",
        "predict -> tokens\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The words are nodes.  Directed edges represent syntactic relations.  The main verb `predict` is connected to subject-like and object-like components.\n",
        "\n",
        "Mathematically, for a sentence with $T$ tokens, a dependency graph has an adjacency matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A\\in \\{0,1\\}^{T\\times T}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If $A_{ij}=1$ means token $i$ points to token $j$, then row $i$ lists outgoing syntactic neighbors.  A graph-based model may update a token representation by aggregating information from its syntactic neighbors rather than from all tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "Dependency graphs are useful because they remove some irrelevant word-distance effects.  In a long sentence, two syntactically related words may be far apart in the sequence but close in the dependency graph.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.6 Word co-occurrence graphs\n",
        "\n",
        "A word co-occurrence graph has one node per word type.  Two words are connected if they occur near each other in a corpus.  A simple construction uses a window size $r$.  For every occurrence of word $w_t$, connect it to words $w_s$ satisfying\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "0 < |s-t| \\le r.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The edge weight may count co-occurrence frequency:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A_{ij}=\\#\\{(s,t): w_s=i, w_t=j, 0<|s-t|\\le r\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "After symmetrization, $A$ becomes an undirected weighted graph.  This graph captures the distributional hypothesis: words appearing in similar neighborhoods tend to have related meanings.\n",
        "\n",
        "This connects graph methods to Chapter 8.  Co-occurrence matrices, PMI matrices, and word graphs are different views of the same empirical object.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.7 Document-term graphs as bipartite graphs\n",
        "\n",
        "A document-term matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X\\in R^{m\\times d}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "can be interpreted as a bipartite graph.  One part contains document nodes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\{D_1,\\ldots,D_m\\},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the other part contains term nodes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\{t_1,\\ldots,t_d\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "There is an edge between document $D_i$ and term $t_j$ with weight $X_{ij}$.  The full adjacency matrix has block form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A_{\\\\text{bip}}=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "0 & X \\\\\n",
        "X^T & 0\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This construction lets information propagate between documents through shared terms and between terms through shared documents.  For example, two documents need not share a direct edge; they may become related through paths of length two:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_i \\to t_j \\to D_k.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The document-document similarity matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "XX^T,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the term-term similarity matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X^T X.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus the familiar Gram matrices from vector-space NLP are also two-step walk matrices in a bipartite graph.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.8 Knowledge graphs\n",
        "\n",
        "A knowledge graph stores triples\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(h,r,t),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $h$ is a head entity, $r$ is a relation, and $t$ is a tail entity.  For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "(Transformer, is_a, neural_architecture)\n",
        "(BERT, uses, self_attention)\n",
        "(NLP, uses, probability)\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "A knowledge graph is not merely a graph; it is usually a labeled directed multigraph.  Different relations should not always be treated as the same type of edge.\n",
        "\n",
        "For $R$ relation types, one may define relation-specific adjacency matrices\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A^{(1)},\\ldots,A^{(R)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The entry $A^{(r)}_{ij}=1$ means that relation $r$ connects entity $i$ to entity $j$.  This creates an adjacency tensor\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal A\\in R^{R\\times n\\times n}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Relation-specific structure is important.  The relation `is_a` is not the same as `part_of`, and neither is the same as `cites` or `teaches`.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.9 Random walks and centrality\n",
        "\n",
        "Graphs support random walks.  For an undirected weighted graph with degree matrix $D$, the row-stochastic transition matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P=D^{-1}A.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If $p_t\\in R^n$ is a row vector distribution over nodes at time $t$, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_{t+1}=p_t P.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A stationary distribution satisfies\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi = \\pi P.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For connected non-bipartite undirected graphs, the stationary distribution is proportional to degree:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi_i = \\frac{d_i}{\\sum_j d_j}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Random walks appear in NLP through ranking, graph-based disambiguation, label propagation, and representation learning.  PageRank modifies the walk by adding teleportation:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_{t+1}=\\alpha p_t P + (1-\\alpha)v,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $v$ is a restart distribution and $0<\\alpha<1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.10 Label propagation\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a graph has a small set of labeled nodes and many unlabeled nodes.  Let $Y\\in R^{n\\times K}$ store label scores.  A simple label propagation iteration is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F^{(k+1)} = \\alpha S F^{(k)} + (1-\\alpha)Y,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $S$ is a normalized adjacency matrix.  At convergence, if $0<\\alpha<1$, the fixed point satisfies\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F^* = \\alpha S F^* + (1-\\alpha)Y,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "so\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F^* = (1-\\alpha)(I-\\alpha S)^{-1}Y,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "provided the inverse exists.\n",
        "\n",
        "This formula shows that label propagation is not magic.  It is repeated averaging over a graph with a restart to the initial labels.\n",
        "\n",
        "In NLP, this can be used to propagate sentiment labels through a word graph, topic labels through a document graph, or resource categories through a knowledge graph.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.11 Message passing as the common abstraction\n",
        "\n",
        "Many graph neural networks use message passing.  Each node $i$ has a hidden state\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_i^{(\\ell)}\\in R^{d_\\ell}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "at layer $\\ell$.  A generic message passing layer has the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "m_i^{(\\ell)} = \\operatorname{AGG}^{(\\ell)}\\left(\\{\\psi^{(\\ell)}(h_i^{(\\ell)},h_j^{(\\ell)},e_{ij}) : j\\in N(i)\\}\\right),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_i^{(\\ell+1)} = \\phi^{(\\ell)}\\left(h_i^{(\\ell)},m_i^{(\\ell)}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $N(i)$ is the neighborhood of node $i$;\n",
        "- $e_{ij}$ may store edge features;\n",
        "- $\\psi$ creates messages;\n",
        "- $\\operatorname{AGG}$ aggregates messages, often by sum, mean, or attention;\n",
        "- $\\phi$ updates the node state.\n",
        "\n",
        "The aggregation should usually be permutation invariant with respect to the ordering of neighbors.  This is the graph analogue of a set function.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.12 A graph convolution layer\n",
        "\n",
        "A common simplified graph convolution layer is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H^{(\\ell+1)} = \\sigma(\\tilde S H^{(\\ell)} W^{(\\ell)}),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $H^{(\\ell)}\\in R^{n\\times d_\\ell}$ stores node features;\n",
        "- $W^{(\\ell)}\\in R^{d_\\ell\\times d_{\\ell+1}}$ is a trainable weight matrix;\n",
        "- $\\sigma$ is a nonlinear activation;\n",
        "- $\\tilde S$ is a normalized adjacency matrix, often built after adding self-loops.\n",
        "\n",
        "With self-loops,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\tilde A = A + I,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\tilde D_{ii}=\\sum_j \\tilde A_{ij},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\tilde S = \\tilde D^{-1/2}\\tilde A\\tilde D^{-1/2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The layer has two operations:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. mix nearby node features by multiplying by $\\tilde S$;\n",
        "2. transform feature coordinates by multiplying by $W^{(\\ell)}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Graph convolution as smoothing plus learning.**  The matrix $\\tilde S$ performs graph smoothing.  The matrix $W^{(\\ell)}$ learns feature combinations.  Their product creates a trainable local averaging operator on graph signals.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.13 Graph attention\n",
        "\n",
        "Self-attention from Chapters 13 and 14 allows every token to attend to every other token.  Graph attention restricts attention to graph neighborhoods.  For node $i$, compute a score for each neighbor $j\\in N(i)$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e_{ij} = a(h_i,h_j).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then normalize over the neighborhood:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\alpha_{ij} = \\frac{\\exp(e_{ij})}{\\sum_{k\\in N(i)}\\exp(e_{ik})}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The update is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_i' = \\sigma\\left(\\sum_{j\\in N(i)} \\alpha_{ij} W h_j\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is attention on a sparse graph.  In NLP, it can be used on dependency graphs, document graphs, entity graphs, and hybrid graphs connecting documents, passages, entities, and concepts.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.14 Relational message passing\n",
        "\n",
        "Knowledge graphs require relation-specific updates.  A simple relational message passing layer is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_i' = \\sigma\\left(\n",
        "W_0 h_i + \\sum_{r=1}^R\\sum_{j\\in N_r(i)} \\frac{1}{c_{i,r}} W_r h_j\n",
        "\\right),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $N_r(i)$ is the set of neighbors connected to $i$ by relation $r$, $W_r$ is a relation-specific matrix, and $c_{i,r}$ is a normalization constant.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is important because edge labels carry meaning.  Treating all relations as identical may lose the distinction between syntactic, semantic, citation, and membership relations.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 23.15 Interactive: graph message passing\n",
        "\n",
        "The following interactive shows a small word graph.  Node colors represent a scalar graph signal.  Each message-passing step replaces a node's value by a convex combination of its current value and the average value of its neighbors:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f^{(k+1)} = (1-\\alpha)f^{(0)} + \\alpha P f^{(k)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The parameter $\\alpha$ controls how strongly information propagates through the graph.\n",
        "\n",
